WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.660
New oceanic lithosphere is hot and thin.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:07.120
Only its relatively shallow, cool part behaves brittlely, so ridge earthquakes

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.260
are generally shallow and small to moderate.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.640
Normal faulting and magma entering fissures generate earthquakes along the ridge

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.880
axis. Strike-slip motion occurs on transform faults connecting adjacent ridge segments.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.420
As a plate moves away from the ridge, cooling, subsidence, and

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.780
bending can activate faults away from the axis.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.160
Not every ridge-system earthquake lies on the spreading axis.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.260
Bending before the trench can fracture the approaching plate.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.700
Thrust faults in the accretionary prism accommodate scraped-off material.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.960
The megathrust is the plate-contact thrust fault.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.100
Large slip and vertical seafloor displacement can produce destructive tsunamis;

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.840
not every subduction earthquake produces one.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.920
The cold descending slab supports intermediate and deep earthquakes, approaching 700

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.700
km in the supplied chapter.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.100
This deep slab seismicity is distinct from a shallow megathrust rupture.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.780
The overriding plate can also have thrusting, magma movement, and volcanic

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:03.440
seismicity.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.780
Continental collision spreads deformation across a broad zone.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:12.200
Thrusting near the boundary and reactivation of faults inside either plate

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.780
can produce earthquakes; strike-slip and normal motion can also accommodate regional

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.180
deformation. Continental rifts commonly generate normal-fault earthquakes.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.980
Continental transform faults generate mostly shallow strike-slip earthquakes, sometimes large ones.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.640
Magma intrusion and renewed stress on old faults provide intraplate mechanisms.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.640
New Madrid illustrates that distance from a modern boundary does not

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.420
make a continent’s interior immune.
